# DuDuClaw OS — A/B GPT layout (Y8-1, 2026-08-27).
#
# Yocto port of the Debian appliance line's mkosi.repart/ four-partition
# design (commercial/docs/DESIGN-ab-update-rollback-2026-08.md §1.1, §11):
# ESP + root-A (populated, active) + root-B (empty, reserved update target)
# + /data (grows to fill the real disk, hosts update staging). The MECHANISM
# is ported verbatim (same partition roles, same GPT type GUID, same label
# scheme, same three attribute bits); the FORM is Yocto-native because wic's
# kickstart-style partitioning language is a different tool with a smaller
# vocabulary than mkosi.repart's declarative `.conf` drop-ins — see
# meta-duduclaw/classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass for the one thing wic
# cannot express in-line (raw GPT attribute bits) and how this build fills
# that gap.
#
# p1 (ESP) is copied byte-for-byte from the already-QEMU-verified single-
# partition efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in (openembedded-core/meta/files/wic/) —
# deliberately unchanged, so the UKI/systemd-boot discovery chain that
# passed Y1-1 stays exactly as it was. Everything from p2 onward is new.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE, NOT AN EDIT TO efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in: this is a
# structurally different, higher-risk partition table (four partitions
# instead of one, GPT attribute post-processing required — see the
# bbclass). Per this project's own established pattern for risky additions
# on top of a verified baseline (duduclaw-image.bb `require`s
# duduclaw-image-minimal.bb rather than editing it in place;
# duduclaw-image-flatpak.bb does the same to duduclaw-image.bb), the A/B
# layout is opted into by a NEW image recipe
# (recipes-core/images/duduclaw-image-ab.bb) that overrides WKS_FILE to
# point here, rather than mutating the wks every existing image recipe
# already depends on and has QEMU evidence for.
#
# SIZES: `DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB` / `DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB` are plain
# bitbake variables (defaults set in duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass,
# overridable per image recipe) — `.wks.in` files go through bitbake's own
# `${VAR}` expansion before wic ever sees them
# (image_types_wic.bbclass:do_write_wks_template, confirmed by reading that
# class, not assumed from the `.in` suffix convention alone). Root-A and
# root-B MUST stay the same size: systemd-sysupdate dd's a slot-A-sized
# payload into slot B with no resize step (same "required for
# systemd-sysupdate" invariant the Debian line's own 20-root-a.conf/
# 21-root-b.conf comments state).
#
# ARCHITECTURE: this file is written for x86_64 only (the
# duduclaw-genericx86-64 / duduclaw-qemux86-64 machines — this layer has no
# arm64 target). The GPT type GUID below
# (4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709) is systemd's
# SD_GPT_ROOT_X86_64 — verified against the actual pinned systemd source
# (src/systemd/sd-gpt.h at SRCREV b3d8fc43e9cb531d958c17ef2cd93b374bc14e8a),
# not copied from the Debian design doc's own arm64-only build without
# re-checking — the Debian design doc's §1.5 forensic dump was against an
# arm64 mkosi artifact (`root-arm64`, GUID b921b045-...) and is NOT the
# right constant to reuse here.

bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=5

# p1: ESP — unchanged from efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in.
part /boot --source bootimg_efi --sourceparams="loader=${EFI_PROVIDER}" --label boot --active --align 1024 --use-uuid --part-name="ESP" --part-type=C12A7328-F81F-11D2-BA4B-00A0C93EC93B --overhead-factor=1

# p2: root-A — the only partition with real content at build time. Label is
# the version-string scheme systemd-sysupdate itself relies on for slot
# selection (sd-boot / gpt-auto's `find_gpt_root()` picks the root partition
# with the highest `strverscmp_improved()` label among non-no-auto,
# native-root-typed partitions — this is NOT cosmetic, it is how "which slot
# is active" is actually decided at runtime). `${DUDUCLAW_PLATFORM_VERSION}`
# is the same single-source-of-truth version this layer already uses for
# DISTRO_VERSION (conf/distro/include/duduclaw-platform-version.inc) — using
# it here means a factory image's own root-A label always matches what
# `os-release`'s `IMAGE_VERSION=` reports, which
# `crates/duduclaw-gateway/src/os_update.rs`'s `ProtectVersion=%A` depends
# on to protect the running slot from ever being treated as a free target.
part / --source rootfs --fstype=ext4 --label "duduclaw-os_${DUDUCLAW_PLATFORM_VERSION}" --align 1024 --exclude-path boot/ --part-name="duduclaw-root-a" --part-type=4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709 --fixed-size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB}

# p3: root-B — the reserved, empty update target. `_empty` is the ONE label
# string systemd-sysupdate's `Type=partition` target recognises as "free to
# write into" (sysupdate.d(5) — this is not a DuDuClaw convention, it is the
# literal string upstream's own partition-target code matches on). Formatted
# ext4 here (unlike the Debian line's raw unformatted slot) purely because
# wic's kickstart partitioning requires SOME --fstype for size/empty-
# partition handling — this filesystem is irrelevant and gets completely
# overwritten the moment sysupdate (or, in a manual test, `dd`) writes a real
# root image into this partition; it carries no content that matters.
# --no-fstab-update: this slot is never mounted at boot (no mountpoint
# argument), so it must never get an /etc/fstab line — an unmountable UUID
# left in fstab would fail the whole mount unit chain on generation.
# GPT attribute bits (NoAuto + ReadOnly, matching the Debian line's
# 21-root-b.conf `NoAuto=yes`/`ReadOnly=yes` factory state) are NOT settable
# in wic's kickstart syntax at all (confirmed: `ksparser.py`'s `part`
# argument list has no equivalent of mkosi.repart's `Flags=`/`NoAuto=`/
# `ReadOnly=`/`GrowFileSystem=`) — set as a post-`do_image_wic` step by
# duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass instead. Until that runs, this partition is
# indistinguishable from an ordinary auto-discoverable root partition, which
# would make gpt-auto-generator's tie-break (`strverscmp_improved` picks the
# higher label) the ONLY thing preventing it from being mounted — correct by
# luck (`_empty` sorts below any real version string, per Debian's own
# §4.1 finding: `_` = 0x5F < `d` = 0x64), but the NoAuto bit is what makes it
# correct on purpose, matching the design doc's own stated defense-in-depth
# reasoning for why NoAuto exists at all (§1.1: "gpt-auto-generator 看到兩個
# 候選 root 會歧義" was a REAL incident on the Debian line before that bit was
# added).
part --fstype=ext4 --label "_empty" --align 1024 --part-name="duduclaw-root-b" --part-type=4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709 --fixed-size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB} --no-fstab-update

# p4: /data — update staging + gateway state, grows to fill whatever is left
# of the real disk (GPT attribute bit 59 / GrowFileSystem, same
# post-processing mechanism as p3's NoAuto/ReadOnly — see the bbclass).
# Setting the bit alone does NOT make growth happen automatically on real
# hardware with a bigger disk than this factory image: that requires
# systemd-repart to actually run against a `repart.d/` drop-in on the
# target, which is NOT wired up yet (tracked as a Y8-1 followup, not done in
# this wave — see PACKAGECONFIG[repart]'s own comment in
# recipes-core/systemd/systemd_%.bbappend for why the binary is present
# anyway). On THIS wks the size is simply fixed at
# DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB — sized for QEMU dev/test disks, not for a real
# mini-PC's full disk.
#
# GPT type is the generic `linux-generic` GUID (0FC63DAF-8483-4772-8E79-
# 3D69D8477DE4), matching the Debian line's own `30-data.conf` (`Type=
# linux-generic`) — /data is explicitly NOT a `root`-typed partition (it
# must never be a gpt-auto root candidate).
part /data --fstype=ext4 --label duduclaw-data --align 1024 --part-name="duduclaw-data" --part-type=0FC63DAF-8483-4772-8E79-3D69D8477DE4 --size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB}
